 first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y = x$ and $x = 1$, and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/40f38ffcf79abe1b11cb7e59b6dd1efb.png)" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128797 1145 "For a plane lamina ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/42913b72caaae9f0740c30760baff88881610a1ced3ff3859222d27d1b8656b4.png) with mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3ee7e6579dec5241a245eab4eaf1b1fa3c180e6ec75a557baa9b2497b55558e2.png) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/84b2f388da9d980669e8b0d6370edb0a12ef82752f16b4fb1b6dc6ee4c44c03d.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/60a59f8ad0379b033d9d2bac9d67afe5c40a43f6d1f2bb6169b90ca1a78221aa.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/00ad0bc1dadfb43a63229d80b11e02988bbe482c5d5122ff28c54bc3d2309231.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/b36595e561b6dadab98447cbca0621828e651a2a3616504357aa90d13a7a20b2.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/4cfac7a7f94c2c55d261fd1e84810bf193920e1003cc0fdc6e841fcb8d8b3f1d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region with representation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e4896170bd06ee1f66dd595e504209977df6611d8c5c4fa0cbf28d2dc146beaf.png)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c1301c17b1dbb8b48716d09ad6f0a7a105917ea7a1c0e942bbe087b12772f254.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3bc1fabeed9c38d36d49380c17ba47c6c0a9d6a1723bed3a4f6e33467bd893e2.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c26b2d7388707b35315aac38bf33a36573a637e3410d1a8e2797fc4164f6d438.png) is a constant of proportionality. \n\nSince the density contains the unknown constant ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/d61c1c56d7c970921acb23214ab2ece4bbd33fefbf212b481ca95fd65fb926df.png), we first use the total mass ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/86f38d0431d36a6b7010ff7611af19c2c54116e9465f15e5a36cf85c933e3054.png) to determine ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/9025c6a94271bd94be0bb930ab14cdb7611034b480b7b56ca76767f957f789c7.png)\n\nWe can find an expression for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e375b825a2d61d973c964706a6bf27c9af8a19b3594f02cdf70afb62b988e66b.png) in terms of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/6e32bbbc4bffbd45f252c9185c7760901b027dd554ef49d044c78e87331029b4.png) by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e4fc94647cb930e64247e9bac85885f6ac4fae1ed132be30f35db0f3723f4f43.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/399765a98ec95ac5bc4deb478463b2787bfa1d6a17a2ea0c9d6f026dd6b79563.png) and hence ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/f1e138092994505279c36db8afdf2e7df102c4efbdb5803515077b076009bf81.png) Therefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/00ad0bc1dadfb43a63229d80b11e02988bbe482c5d5122ff28c54bc3d2309231.png)-axis is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3a0e13d8b9068b72cc88b446cfa2fa0c3ac418b9001013a0f630cb5d621b3f6a.png)" 13194139535742 true] [17592186128797 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/60a078ad0268df77d16370adbb8a2cfa.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = k \\mid y \\mid = k y\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}kydydx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{x}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2}∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = 6M$ and hence\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = ky = 6M y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)x^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}x^{2} \\cdot ydydx \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{x}x^{2}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= 3M[\\frac{1}{5}x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3M}{5}[x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3M}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128798 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/12fb5f07885b074f983cab3a5fba0faff607d8aa34d7cd3abe4e94abf53dac25.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139535742 true] [17592186128798 1100 "Consider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}$. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/fe53edf914e85df412be64f421b172c0.png)" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128798 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot r^{3}\\cos^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/4}∫_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot r^{3}\\cos^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/4}∫_{1}^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= (∫_{1}^{2}rdr)(∫_{π/6}^{π/4}dθ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{2} \\mid _{1}^{2} \\cdot θ \\mid _{π/6}^{π/4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - 1) \\cdot (\\frac{π}{4} - \\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128798 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186128965 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/de882e240d0a6ca824e77397fe9c6b51eb94d36c2e5e3524422ef0a0f28103de.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/48c72d7f57bb7f3163fa37903a032773c12ddce6fdc0e0bee8818259aba9e465.png) and uniform mass density occupies the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) enclosed between the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/54d73239bada862f9b8d6d3d7a4f73bea15432668f6b2116098faa02a4b3d435.png)-axis and the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3879c9475dcb86235e269108d73767f2e631eee68e65aacbfffbc22ada2033e1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/52ab6e6677e9404e8b08bfff7b90cbfb332b344d4fe8ecc19b603ef05616ebf7.png) as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/f0a3d06d2c55c6889a22a1cc4e11adb35b71e24e1395e6d6048c1135620c225f.png)-axis." 13194139535742 true] [17592186128965 1100 "A triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2-x$, as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/ae2c087be0a73aeee4dc4137c925a39b.png)" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128965 1145 "For a plane lamina ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/28390f00cdb34a228868c36035ff66f1ea65bf7ff5e592fa473fca18d6addbcc.png) with mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c97ba2315cf57f0ca52513a553203387b121396b6b0a5c96fc9a67e0ae6798d4.png) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/b8379c5ab1146e79464ee5677bc05edac3c6a9368629746b08c9a9e66109681c.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/4cf2e90080f1ae459e72ac253d6617d0defdcc3be58a1ca385bddc3215034eca.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/ac1949df7033d3a968286404f658fea6ad7de10f446d6ba8f4090bf40fa26afa.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/509782164a1c349c9ba8b6399b252decae9b022ffde15d840bc68b123872071d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) is a type I region with representation\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e666ae60e603116812051833c70d32199e3f146e23fc99260c274fb546bfeef8.png)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/68904cbee1839a61f4d65c5ff03066d8f28b8a5e070f6ddb00c82c29cfd93fcd.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/eebd5724c98ea00681ef901dabae1d0b3428aff0c6e6a9cdadc020ed46f129c7.png) is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/0285f4ab3e7a9c974f244d0ab517ca737d2a5aec35bfbceb779037d54ce00f98.png)\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/d3089c43ce61bf64a57a6d64b38987b91a6c8f9aa9f06d106f4c124ed430dd09.png)\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/235efd30dbd8ca8883230793c8b79882afd19443c612b54f30b5cd507a646c9c.png)-axis is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/148b58f76868ac2f7a85e68627f91f2fa47c6c6a2bd5bcb67af8ba8af011167a.png)" 13194139535742 true] [17592186128965 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2-x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/1ca849c74b98783c11e88da02f294af8.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 2\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{x} &= ∬_(D)y^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{2}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{2 - x}y^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{2}∫_{0}^{2}[∫_{0}^{2 - x}y^{2}dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{2}∫_{0}^{2}[\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2 - x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{6}∫_{0}^{2}(2 - x)^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{6}[- \\frac{1}{4}(2 - x)^{4}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{M}{24}[(2 - x)^{4}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{M}{24}(0^{4} - 2^{4}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{M}{24} \\cdot (-16) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128966 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/46d6a57700d1df7f2de9a46507821a8553876eb956be6ef87a455d5ca0fabafb.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139535742 true] [17592186128966 1100 "A plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy$, find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/72446ce285072cf28a0dcd840a3d6893.png)" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128966 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/085cf7b65bcf36333d18ac045baf3641.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{x} &= ∬_(D)y^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{x^{2}}^{1}y^{2} \\cdot 4xydydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{x^{2}}^{1}4xy^{3}dy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[xy^{4}]_{y = x^{2}}^{y = 1}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x(1 - x^{8})dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x - x^{9}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{x^{10}}{10}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 false] [17592186128966 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_x =  \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_y =  \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/8846da0fe3b1e8369a9277bbaba1e082d040136a71105a5816af7b13d2788027.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{10}}{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186129146 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364411 13194139535742 true] [17592186129146 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364418 13194139535742 true] [17592186129146 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364425 13194139535742 true] [17592186129146 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364432 13194139535742 true] [17592186129147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364362 13194139535742 true] [17592186129147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364369 13194139535742 true] [17592186129147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364376 13194139535742 true] [17592186129147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364390 13194139535742 true] [17592186129219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364383 13194139535742 true] [17592186129219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364397 13194139535742 true] [17592186129219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364404 13194139535742 true] [17592186129220 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364439 13194139535742 true] [17592186129220 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364446 13194139535742 true] [17592186129220 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364453 13194139535742 true] [17592186129220 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364460 13194139535742 true] [17592186129220 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364467 13194139535742 true] [17592186129466 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126602 13194139535742 false] [17592186129466 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364350 13194139535742 true] [17592186129466 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535742 true] [17592186129466 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139535742 true] [17592186129466 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $Mkg$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 2}$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139535742 false] [17592186129466 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{5M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186129468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126614 13194139535742 false] [17592186129468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364356 13194139535742 true] [17592186129468 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535742 true] [17592186129468 1100 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$\nwith mass density function $λ(x, y) = (6 - xy)kg/m^{2}$, where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." 13194139535742 false] [17592186129468 1100 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(6-xy)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." 13194139535742 true] [17592186129468 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(6-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+6{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}y+6x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}16-4y+12{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[16y-2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =48-18+108-\\frac{81}{2}-0 \\\\  & =97.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $97.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186129470 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535742 true] [17592186129470 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$.\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139535742 false] [17592186129470 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139535742 true] [17592186129470 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}-0)(3-0) \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=M{x}^{2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{5}+3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{18M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186129471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535742 true] [17592186129471 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c290abf6acdff985b9c4aae6a998d7607b251a28285c9a8c15ff7c672e4ddc59.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y=2-x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the sum of its distance from the $x$-axis and its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139535742 true] [17592186129471 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/q-162987.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y = 2 - x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the sum of its distance from the $x$-axis and its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139535742 false] [17592186129471 1145 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/50ee44f265b3b27d7751e47a142053e06ee444b42481cd1277b5dacbb3ba8583.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k(|x|+|y|)=k(x+y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}k(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}x+y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2-x)+\\frac{1}{2}(2-x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}2-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[2x-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{8}$ and hence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k(x+y)=\\frac{3M}{8}(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}x(2-x)^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}(2-x)^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{12}(2-x)^{3}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-64x+48\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}{[-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+8{x}^{3}-32{x}^{2}+48x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}(-\\frac{32}{5}+64-128+96) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}\\cdot \\frac{128}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186364350 1000 #edb/ref 