ambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364462 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364463 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364464 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364465 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364466 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461 1041 "selection" 13194139535742 true] [17592186364461